--TEST--
`Core\Zip` lists an archive's entries and reads one by name, and every way that can fail is a `ParseError` naming what the archive did — `rule:core-api/tier-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `notes/one.txt` holding `first` and `notes/two.txt` holding `second`, both
// stored rather than deflated.
bytes $ordinary = Core\Encoding::fromBase64("");

// A name comes out of an archive somebody else wrote, so it is `tainted`
// whatever the archive's own type was — the same reading a claim out of a
// verified token gets. A literal archive in a test is no safer than a
// downloaded one, so there is no form of this call that answers a plain
// `string`.
foreach (Core\Zip::entries($ordinary) as tainted string $name) {
    echo "entry: ", $name, "\n";
}

// A name is compared whole rather than resolved as a path, so an entry is read
// by exactly the spelling `entries` answered.
tainted bytes $two = Core\Zip::read($ordinary, "notes/two.txt");
tainted string $text = $two as string;
echo "notes/two.txt: ", Core\Bytes::length($two), " octets, holding \"", $text, "\"\n";

// A deflated entry reads the same way. `notes/one.txt` here is 1000 octets of
// text in 15 octets of deflate, well inside the shipped bound.
bytes $deflated = Core\Encoding::fromBase64("UEsDBBQAAAAIAAAAAAC4p6JdDwAAAOgDAAANAAAAbm90ZXMvb25lLnR4dEvLLCouSRslRolRYjgTAFBLAQIeAxQAAAAIAAAAAAC4p6JdDwAAAOgDAAANAAAAAAAAAAAAAACkgQAAAABub3Rlcy9vbmUudHh0UEsFBgAAAAABAAEAOwAAADoAAAAAAA==");
echo "a deflated entry: ", Core\Bytes::length(Core\Zip::read($deflated, "notes/one.txt")), " octets\n";

// Octets that are not an archive are refused as what they are, rather than
// answering an empty listing a caller would read as "this archive is empty".
try {
    Core\Zip::entries("not an archive at all" as bytes);
    echo "octets that are not an archive listed\n";
} catch (ParseError $nothing) {
    echo $nothing->message, "\n";
}

// A name no entry carries is refused rather than answered as empty octets.
try {
    Core\Zip::read($ordinary, "notes/three.txt");
    echo "a name no entry carries was read\n";
} catch (ParseError $missing) {
    echo $missing->message, "\n";
}

// `notes/one.txt`, written with method 99 — a compression this class does not
// read. What it does *not* do is hand back the compressed octets.
bytes $method = Core\Encoding::fromBase64("UEsDBBQAAABjAAAAAABX7nGSBQAAAAUAAAANAAAAbm90ZXMvb25lLnR4dGZpcnN0UEsBAh4DFAAAAGMAAAAAAFfucZIFAAAABQAAAA0AAAAAAAAAAAAAAKSBAAAAAG5vdGVzL29uZS50eHRQSwUGAAAAAAEAAQA7AAAAMAAAAAAA");
try {
    Core\Zip::read($method, "notes/one.txt");
    echo "an unreadable method was read\n";
} catch (ParseError $unsupported) {
    echo $unsupported->message, "\n";
}

// The same archive with its deflate stream overwritten: the entry is listed,
// because the central directory is intact, and reading it refuses.
bytes $corrupt = Core\Encoding::fromBase64("UEsDBBQAAAAIAAAAAABX7nGSBwAAAAUAAAANAAAAbm90ZXMvb25lLnR4dP////8uAQBQSwECHgMUAAAACAAAAAAAV+5xkgcAAAAFAAAADQAAAAAAAAAAAAAApIEAAAAAbm90ZXMvb25lLnR4dFBLBQYAAAAAAQABADsAAAAyAAAAAAA=");
echo "a corrupt entry still lists: ", Core\Arr::count(Core\Zip::entries($corrupt)), "\n";
try {
    Core\Zip::read($corrupt, "notes/one.txt");
    echo "a corrupt stream was read\n";
} catch (ParseError $broken) {
    echo $broken->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
entry: notes/one.txt
entry: notes/two.txt
notes/two.txt: 6 octets, holding "second"
a deflated entry: 1000 octets
Core\Zip: not a well-formed zip archive: it has no end-of-central-directory record.
Core\Zip::read(): the archive has no entry named "notes/three.txt". `Core\Zip::entries()` is the list, and a name is compared whole rather than resolved as a path.
Core\Zip will not read an entry: it is compressed by method 99, and this class reads stored and deflate: "notes/one.txt".
a corrupt entry still lists: 1
Core\Zip cannot read an entry: its deflate stream is not well formed ("notes/one.txt").
